      {
        "id": 105863,
        "name": "Ameena",
        "username": "Ameena",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/ameena/{size}/1907_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-06-07T17:13:25.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Yeah, once you've done everything applicable on a card at a given moment in time, you move onto the next card and don't go back to the previous one even if conditions change. So yeah, an unflipped Voss plays Forced Deployment. You kill the Minions during the following round (or the environment does - either way, they die before Voss's next turn). Voss therefore has zero Minions in play at the start of his next turn and as per his character card, flips. The only Start phase action listed on his flipped side is the \"if he has 2+ Minions in play, flip back\" one, which obviously isn't applicable right now as he has none (hence the fact he just flipped). So tht's his card dealt with - you move onto the next one, Forced Deployment (assuming neither of the ships are in play between that and Voss, of course). This destroys itself and all the Minions come back. None of those have a Start phase action so you move straight onto the Play phase, then during the End phase get battered by Voss and all the Minions. Yay :P.</p><p>It's why I like to try and destroy Forced Deployment as early as possible after it enters play - if the first hero of the round has ongoing destruction, you can take it out and bring all the Minons back right then, meaning you have the rest of that hero's turn and all the other heroes (and the environment) to deal with them, before Voss's turn rolls around again and you get mauled. Not so bad if there are few Minions around, of course, or if their damage type/target(s) are easy enough to deal with (for example, a cold/fire Minion going for Ab'Zero, or a two-damage Minion attacking a Driving Mantis-equipped Mr Fixer), but yeah, it's frequently nasty.</p>",
        "post_number": 4,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T03:35:36.547Z",
